soon as you can and  support as gopher Hardy the documentary  I am NOT your negro is equally important  to watch right now and extremely  powerful if any white man in the world  says give me liberty or give me death  the entire white world applauds when a  black man says exactly the same thing  he is judge of criminal and she did like  one and everything possible is done to  make an example of this bad so there  won't be any more like him this feels  more like a film essay rather than a  straight documentary it uses images and  archive clips and narration of author  James Baldwin's work just after the  deaths of his friends Medgar Evers  Malcolm X and Martin Luther King jr.  when James Baldwin died in 1987 he left  just 30 pages of this book but director  rau Peck has imagined what his completed  work might feel like you also get to see  Baldwin speak in old clips like his  address to Cambridge University where  his fierce intelligence is on full  display there are days when you wonder  what your role is in this country and  what your future is in it the parallels  between the civil rights movement of the  60s and the black lives matter movement  right now impossible to ignore and it's  really only by delving into the past  that we can hope to change the future so  please see I am NOT your negro if it's  playing near you it'll give you plenty  to think about and it's out in limited  theaters this weekend another film  speaking about race in America which is  out this week is called chapter and  verse and it's the directorial debut of  Jamal Joseph now he is an in  recive man he is a filmmaker he's an  author he is a film professor he's an  activist and he's a former member of the  Black Panthers and he actually went to  jail for his involvement with that  organization  chapter and verse is a very small  low-budget indie which tells a story of  an ex-con trying to live a straight and  narrow life in Harlem he comes across a  young boy who's starting to get into the  